Dear Hossein,

first off, this is definitely closer to what you want than the models in
your original post. Below you say " also ran a model with NP as a factor too
and got the following result". What does "NP" mean here? As an aside, it is
helpful when you post to post the full model output (incl) the fixed effect
correlations AND the code used for coding as well as the output of the
contrast commands. E.g. what is contrasts(B)? The actual meaning of B1 and
B2 will depend on how you coded B to begin with. For example, if you used
string values to code B, the Helmert coding interpretation will depend on
the alphabetical order of the levels of the factor B.

But based on the assumption that B was coded numerically as 1, 2, 3 and you
then turned it into a factor, your contrast matrix should look like this:

> contrasts(B)
  [,1] [,2]
1   -1   -1
2    1   -1
3    0    2

If we further assume that A was contrast coded as 1 vs. -1 (although I
recommend the same as what Ian wrote above), your first model shows

   - a significant main effect of A, so that the DV is smaller for A = 1
   - level 1 and 2 of B differ significantly, in that the DV is smaller for
   level 2 of B than for level 1 of B
   - level 3 is not significantly different from the mean of levels 1&2 of B
   (although it's approaching significance)
   - the difference between level 1 and 2 of B is ameliorated for A = 1
   (assuming complete balance and the coding assumed above, the effect is
   actually reversed); for A = -1, the contrast between level 1 and 2 of B is
   increased.
   - the difference between level 3 vs. 1&2 of B is negative for A = 1 and
   positive for A = -1 (i.e. the predicted mean of the DV for level 3 is larger
   than the predicted mean of the DV for level 1&2 if A= - 1 and it is smaller
   if A =1)


